# Places of F_N above j=∞ are the poles of j

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.restrict_eq_jLinePlaceInfty_iff`
(`42ee546b-e40b-52b8-8589-1ebff926f651`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `d237ebb6-cb86-5259-9c06-2021cc8b647e`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Fix $N \ge 1$ and equip the field $F_N = \mathbb{Q}(j_q, j_q(q^N))$ — the intermediate field
[`ModularCurve.modularFunctionField N`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L250) of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq
\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the Laurent series $j_q = q^{-1}\cdot(\text{power
series } jNumQ)$ together with its image under $q \mapsto q^N$ — with the $\mathbb{Q}\langle
j_q\rangle$-algebra structure given by the inclusion of subfields. Assume $F_N$ is integral over
$\mathbb{Q}\langle j_q\rangle$, and let $w$ be a place of $F_N$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, i.e. a valuation
subring of $F_N$ containing the image of $\mathbb{Q}$, distinct from $F_N$, and a principal ideal
ring. Then the restriction of $w$ to $\mathbb{Q}\langle j_q\rangle$ (the preimage valuation subring
along the inclusion) equals [`ModularCurve.jLinePlaceInfty`](def/ModularCurve_JLinePlaces.html#L58)
— the place at infinity of $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,\mathbb{Q}$ transported along the isomorphism
$\mathrm{RatFunc}\,\mathbb{Q} \cong \mathbb{Q}\langle j_q\rangle$ coming from the transcendence of
$j_q$ — if and only if $\mathrm{ord}_w$ of the element $j_q$ of $F_N$ is negative, where
$\mathrm{ord}_w$ is minus the logarithm of the adic valuation attached to $w$.

This identifies the places of the function field of $X_0(N)$ lying above the point $j = \infty$ of
the $j$-line as exactly those at which $j$ has a pole, the cusps. It is used in the computations of
the numbers of elliptic points, in
[`ModularCurve.natCard_ord_jBar_eq_one_eq_nuThree`]
(thm.html#ModularCurve.natCard_ord_jBar_eq_one_eq_nuThree)
and
[`ModularCurve.natCard_ord_jBar_sub_1728_eq_one_eq_nuTwo`]
(thm.html#ModularCurve.natCard_ord_jBar_sub_1728_eq_one_eq_nuTwo),
where places above $j = \infty$ must be separated from those above $j = 0$ and $j = 1728$.

